openssl-provider-ed521 for Debian
---------------------------------

The provider verifies Ed521 signatures (EdDSA over E-521, OID
1.3.6.1.4.1.44588.2.1), the algorithm of ICP-Brasil's root certificate v7.
It cannot generate keys or sign.

How it is configured
--------------------

libcrypto reads /usr/lib/ssl/openssl.cnf, which the openssl package ships as
a link to /etc/ssl/openssl.cnf.  Debian's openssl has no directory for
configuration snippets, so this package diverts that link:

  /usr/lib/ssl/openssl.cnf          -> /usr/share/openssl-provider-ed521/openssl.cnf
  /usr/lib/ssl/openssl.cnf.distrib  -> /etc/ssl/openssl.cnf  (Debian's link)

/usr/share/openssl-provider-ed521/openssl.cnf includes /etc/ssl/openssl.cnf
first, so your settings there still apply, then adds the provider ed521 to
[provider_sect] and activates the default provider in [default_sect]: once
any provider is activated, OpenSSL no longer loads the default provider by
itself (config(5), "Default provider and its activation").

The package switches the link only after checking, at installation, that
the new configuration activates exactly the providers active before plus
ed521, that the default provider is among them, that no FIPS provider is
active, that SHA-256 works and that ITI's v7 root verifies.  Otherwise it
leaves the configuration as it was and says why.

  /usr/libexec/openssl-provider-ed521/system-config status

shows the state.  As root, "system-config disable" puts Debian's link back
and keeps it so across upgrades; "system-config enable" switches it on
again.  Removing the package puts Debian's link back too.

What changes for programs
-------------------------

Programs that read the system configuration (openssl, curl, Python's ssl,
Qt) can verify Ed521 certificates, CRLs and CMS signatures.  Every other
algorithm is answered by the providers that answered it before.  A program
that loads providers itself (OSSL_PROVIDER_load) now finds the default
provider already active, as with any configuration that activates it.

Programs that use NSS (Firefox, Chromium, Okular's NSS backend), GnuPG
(Kleopatra, KMail, Okular's GnuPG backend), GnuTLS or Java do not use
OpenSSL's providers and cannot verify Ed521 through this package.

To configure it by hand instead, run "system-config disable" and add these
lines to /etc/ssl/openssl.cnf:

  [provider_sect]
  ed521 = ed521_sect
  [default_sect]
  activate = 1
  [ed521_sect]
  activate = 1
